Acute Limb Ischemia (Lower)
Acute Urgent Care
Excruciating foot/calf pain, Absent lower limb pulses, Acute foot drop/anesthesia

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AUC-009
Reason for Visit: Sudden, excruciating pain in the left lower leg accompanied by coldness, loss of feeling, and inability to move the foot.
Location: Nursing home
Present at Visit: Patient and facility nursing staff
Urgency Justification: The nursing staff reported an acute onset of severe, unremitting calf and foot pain with associated pallor and an absent pedal pulse via handheld Doppler. Urgent on-site assessment was mandated to confirm acute arterial occlusion. Bypassing the after-hours triage to wait for the morning GP round would have pushed the patient beyond the viability window for limb salvage, resulting in gangrene and requiring a major amputation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My left leg is completely dead and freezing, and the pain in my calf is unbearable.
History of Present Illness: The patient experienced the sudden onset of acute, excruciating pain in the left foot and calf two hours ago. The pain is unremitting and not relieved by rest or standard facility analgesia. The patient describes a profound sensory loss beginning as toe paresthesia and rapidly progressing to complete anesthesia of the foot. The patient also reports a sudden inability to actively wiggle the toes. A history of pre-existing intermittent claudication and peripheral arterial disease is documented in the nursing home chart, suggesting an in-situ thrombosis as the likely etiology.
Functional Status: Bedbound; previously mobilized with a walker.
Social/Safety Concerns: High dependency on nursing staff; patient is highly distressed.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 150/85 | HR: 92 | Temp: 36.4 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 95% | Wt: 71 Kg
General: Patient is writhing in bed, clutching the left thigh, and groaning in severe pain.
Physical Exam: The left foot and lower leg exhibit profound pallor and early non-blanching mottling. There is an icy cold skin temperature starting at the toes and extending proximally to the mid-calf, showing a clear line of demarcation. Femoral pulse is weak but palpable; however, popliteal, dorsalis pedis, and posterior tibial pulses are completely absent on palpation and inaudible via hand-held Doppler. Neurological examination reveals complete anesthesia of the foot and motor paralysis characterized by a severe foot drop. The calf muscle compartments are rigid and exquisitely tender, indicating advanced ischaemia. Expected CT angiography would reveal an abrupt cut-off in the superficial femoral or popliteal artery.
Environmental Observations: Nursing home bed adjusted; legs currently elevated by staff (corrected immediately).
Medication Reconciliation: Patient is on Clopidogrel and Atorvastatin.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Limb Ischemia (Lower) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the profound pallor, absent Doppler signals, and perishing coldness, contrasting with the warmth, swelling, and preserved pulses typical of DVT.

Sciatica/Lumbar Radiculopathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to the objective loss of arterial pulses and the cold, mottled appearance of the skin, which confirm vascular rather than purely neuropathic pathology.

Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of erythema, heat, and fever, as well as the acute, sudden onset of symptoms.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits end-stage signs of acute lower limb ischemia: severe pain, pulselessness, pallor, coldness, and critical neurological deficits (foot drop and complete anesthesia). The rigid, tender calf suggests impending muscle necrosis and potential early compartment syndrome. An urgent after-hours evaluation was absolutely critical to physically assess the viability of the limb and to immediately coordinate an emergency ambulance transfer. A delay of even a few hours for a routine medical review would have permanently closed the window for revascularization, cementing the need for a major, life-altering amputation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Instructed nursing staff to immediately place the left leg in a dependent position (dangling off the bed). Administered Morphine 5mg subcutaneously for severe pain. Called 000 for Priority 1 transport to a hospital with vascular surgery capabilities.
Positive Behavioral Change: By reporting any future sudden leg pain immediately to the nursing staff without waiting to see if it improves, you ensure the medical team can respond fast enough to save the tissue before permanent damage occurs.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Ambulance transport.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover to paramedics; notify nursing home physician of transfer.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance dispatched. The nursing staff are instructed not to elevate the leg and not to apply heating pads or compression stockings under any circumstances while waiting for transport.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The blood supply to your leg is completely blocked. This is a surgical emergency that requires immediate hospital imaging and an operation.
Clinical Course: Without immediate restoration of blood flow, the tissue in your leg will die. The hospital team will work to clear the blockage or bypass it.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Keep your leg hanging off the side of the bed. Do not let anyone elevate it, as gravity is the only thing helping blood reach your foot right now.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: I have given you a strong injection of morphine to control the pain. We have lowered your leg so that gravity can pull as much blood down past the blockage as possible while we wait for the ambulance.
Actionable Steps: Try to remain as still as possible to keep your heart rate steady. Focus on deep breathing while the nurses pack your transfer documents.

